#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175637 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ef56d-869d-5939-9a0a-84477cf5a074" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Polar Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/id #uuid "45ec3318-742d-582c-bdf1-d9f35f0bd99c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12561" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$, given by\n\n$\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: r^2, \\: 2 r \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n$y=4{x^2}+\\dfrac{z^2}{4}$.\nBy first considering the transformations\n$x =\\dfrac1{2}X, \\qquad y = Z, \\qquad z = 2Y$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$." :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx =\\dfrac1{2}X, \\qquad y = Z, \\qquad z = 2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ = X^2 + Y^2\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad Y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad Z = z\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nZ &= X^{2} + Y^{2} \\\\\nZ &= (r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2} \\\\\nZ &= r^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) \\\\\nZ &= r^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r, θ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}X, Z, 2Y⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}r\\cos θ, Z, 2r\\sin θ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}r\\cos θ, r^{2}, 2r\\sin θ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: r \\in [0,\\infty)]$ and $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).]$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue} \\dfrac1{2} r \\cos\\theta}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175743 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10bfbafe-b8d7-57d8-9dcb-b84b85328620" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of One Sheet"}] :question/id #uuid "6745a851-eaa4-50a0-b76a-3d847284c586" :question/math-academy-id "e-8886" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$, given by\n\n$\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle 3\\cosh{\\phi} \\cos\\theta, \\: 4\\sinh{\\phi}, \\: \\text{______} \\big\\rangle$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$\\dfrac{x^2}{9}-\\dfrac{y^2}{16}+z^2=1$.\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n$x = 3X, \\qquad y = 4Z, \\qquad z = Y$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $r^2-Z^2 = k^2$ can be parametrized as $\\langle r, Z\\rangle = \\langle k\\cosh\\phi, k\\sinh \\phi\\rangle$* for $\\phi\\in \\mathbb R$." :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx = 3X, \\qquad y = 4Z, \\qquad z = Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nX^2 + Y^2 - Z^2 = 1\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad Y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad Z = Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX^{2} + Y^{2} - Z^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n(r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2} - Z^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nr^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) - Z^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nr^{2} - Z^{2} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola\n\n$$\nr^2 - Z^2 = 1\n$$\n\nrepresents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta$ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n\\langle r, Z \\rangle = \\langle \\cosh{\\phi}, \\: \\sinh{\\phi} \\rangle, \\qquad \\phi \\in \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3X, 4Z, Y⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3r\\cos θ, 4Z, r\\sin θ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3(\\cosh ϕ)\\cos θ, 4\\sinh ϕ, (\\cosh ϕ)\\sin θ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3\\cosh ϕ\\cos θ, 4\\sinh ϕ, \\cosh ϕ\\sin θ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)]$ and $\\phi \\in \\mathbb R$.\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} \\cosh{\\phi} \\sin\\theta}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175746 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece6a5a-e9b2-5531-85f0-3774d0e3f966" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of Two Sheets"}] :question/id #uuid "2b8d5d04-51f1-5394-931a-384d88692ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-8890" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$, given by\n$\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 3\\sinh{\\phi} \\sin\\theta, \\: 3\\cosh{\\phi} \\big\\rangle$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = -9$. By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $z^2-r^2 = k^2$ can be parametrized as $\\langle z,r\\rangle = \\langle k\\cosh\\phi, k\\sinh \\phi\\rangle$* for $\\phi\\in \\mathbb R$." :question/worked-solution "If $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad z = z\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} - z^{2} &= -9 \\\\\n(r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2} - z^{2} &= -9 \\\\\nr^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) - z^{2} &= -9 \\\\\nr^{2} - z^{2} &= -9 \\\\\nz^{2} - r^{2} &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola\n\n$$\nz^2 - r^2 = 9\n$$\n\nrepresents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3597/Source/Images/875299fe0aa7ace760480a264f00ffdb.png)\n\nUsing the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n\\langle z, r \\rangle = \\langle 3\\cosh{\\phi}, \\: 3\\sinh{\\phi} \\rangle, \\qquad \\phi \\in \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨(3\\sinh ϕ)\\cos θ, (3\\sinh ϕ)\\sin θ, 3\\cosh ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3\\sinh ϕ\\cos θ, 3\\sinh ϕ\\sin θ, 3\\cosh ϕ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)]$ and $\\phi \\in \\mathbb R$.\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}3\\sinh{\\phi} \\cos\\theta}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175747 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "140679a6-6a14-5f92-9a92-68917f197863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/id #uuid "84dd50d8-12e0-51d2-90e4-4e602c2e3bb8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8891" :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(y, z) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(y, z)$ above parametrizes the hyperbolic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $-3x=-6y^2+3z^2$. What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" :question/worked-solution "Solving our equation for $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3x=-6y^2+3z^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=2y^2-z^2\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(y, z) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2y^{2} - z^{2}, y, z⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} 2y^2-z^2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10bfbafe-b8d7-57d8-9dcb-b84b85328620" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of One Sheet"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8191ac19-4573-52c9-bc68-084798174975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173371} {:answer/id #uuid "9406da13-2e40-59e4-b026-1da5c137b468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173372} {:answer/id #uuid "db5dd115-ceb3-5aa0-a503-a50b9294e516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sinh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173373} {:answer/id #uuid "30d4873e-9a13-5fe6-b2a0-8e88f0782aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sinh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173374} {:answer/id #uuid "57f7282c-aa74-56bc-bebd-3e3f3e62407c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sinh ϕ\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9406da13-2e40-59e4-b026-1da5c137b468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173372} :answer-field/id #uuid "fed71cfe-2e6e-5677-b3a0-942b5d08c97a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173370}] :question/id #uuid "736a4dfd-f4fe-5ef5-9a6f-9cd95b3ae752" :question/math-academy-id "q-104007" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(θ, ϕ)$, given by\n\n$f(θ, ϕ) = ⟨4\\cosh ϕ\\cos θ, _____, 4\\sinh ϕ⟩$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^{2} + y^{2} - z^{2} = 16$. By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $r^{2} - z^{2} = k^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r, z⟩ = ⟨k\\cosh ϕ, k\\sinh ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ ∈ R$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10bfbafe-b8d7-57d8-9dcb-b84b85328620" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of One Sheet"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c4a20b8-1628-5624-9d4d-bc8bfb1abac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cosh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173377} {:answer/id #uuid "d377433c-9727-58ef-9fa6-578f12dcb1e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cosh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173378} {:answer/id #uuid "1eece7c2-fbc6-5bb0-bcd9-5180b9654e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173379} {:answer/id #uuid "35f3a676-e9f6-5426-acab-cdf85d1b741d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sinh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173380} {:answer/id #uuid "63417fdb-5252-5595-88de-866888d6396a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c4a20b8-1628-5624-9d4d-bc8bfb1abac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cosh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173377} :answer-field/id #uuid "818d02f1-d982-5b13-8b21-52df91f0046c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173376}] :question/id #uuid "5c824c2a-5ae5-5a01-bf81-c7a30b4783b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-104008" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(θ, ϕ)$, given by\n\n$f(θ, ϕ) = ⟨_____, \\sinh ϕ, 2\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ⟩$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$\\frac{x^{2}}{9} - y^{2} + \\frac{z^{2}}{4} = 1$.\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n$x = 3X, y = Z, z = 2Y$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $r^{2} - Z^{2} = k^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r, Z⟩ = ⟨k\\cosh ϕ, k\\sinh ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ ∈ R$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "140679a6-6a14-5f92-9a92-68917f197863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2e1c2a5-35cf-5837-8936-351b8829b474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173407} {:answer/id #uuid "e8834a3e-4981-56db-a311-1331172023c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y^{2} + \\frac{2}{5}z^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173408} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c0ade1-f68d-5f11-9bec-61f3fa0cf482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2y^{2} + \\frac{2}{5}z^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173409} {:answer/id #uuid "350b7ada-87c1-525f-9fe7-801cc17ade9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{9y^{2} + 2z^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173410} {:answer/id #uuid "71d99896-f4d9-5abd-b7ba-d4cefe5e5180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9y^{2} + 2z^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8834a3e-4981-56db-a311-1331172023c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y^{2} + \\frac{2}{5}z^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173408} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ae8d235-e5d3-517b-b520-ee806674a5e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173406}] :question/id #uuid "035095fb-0212-56f5-82b4-682cce36fab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104321" :question/problem "$f(y, z) = ⟨_____, y, z⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y, z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $5x = 10y^{2} + 2z^{2}$. What is the first component of $f$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ef56d-869d-5939-9a0a-84477cf5a074" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Polar Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c11fe839-cbcd-555d-b166-773cdc1649c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{r}{2}\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174601} {:answer/id #uuid "dd89f4aa-0270-5a5f-b4ab-cd9b7c96e1e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174602} {:answer/id #uuid "e83b7b85-791c-5983-bbe6-5a063b12acde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174603} {:answer/id #uuid "fffcd007-d688-54f8-a88d-0cfde7559fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{r}{2}\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174604} {:answer/id #uuid "41ba0e1a-3aae-5bb4-9d23-6479d6e3f103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd89f4aa-0270-5a5f-b4ab-cd9b7c96e1e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174602} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b5d50f5-9972-59d3-8e76-508b9881b689" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174600}] :question/id #uuid "857271c4-29f7-50af-9c53-ede1aa45db8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164541" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r, θ)$, given by\n\n$f(r, θ) = ⟨r^{2}, 2r\\cos θ, _____⟩$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n$x = \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + z^{2}$.\nBy first considering the transformations\n$x = Z, y = 2X, z = Y$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f$."}]}
